<title>BELGIUM: Australia sees progress on EU veterinary pact.</title>
<headline>Australia sees progress on EU veterinary pact.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1996-11-20</dateline>
<text>
<p>Australian Primary Industries and Energy Minister John Anderson said on Wednesday that progress was being made towards a veterinary and plant standards agreement with the European Union.</p>
<p>Speaking after talks with Farm Commissioner Franz Fischler, Anderson said he did not believe trade in these products would be blocked if no agreement was reached before the EU introduces common import rules on January 1, 1997.</p>
<p>&quot;I don't believe there will be a stop in trade. The question is whether there will be some impediments,&quot; Anderson told a news briefing.</p>
<p>He said the outstanding problems were not substantial, adding that Australia accepted the EU's demand to regionalise animal and plant health measures.</p>
<p>&quot;We are in principle prepared to accept that concept. We practice it in Australia,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>Australia exports an estimated 450 million Ecus worth of animal and plant products to the EU annually.</p>
</text>
